                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Scrolls of the Lord is a dark fantasy action title that wears its inspirations proudly. Developed and published by Arbatskii Anatolii, the game presents players with a grim tale of resurrection and redemption in a world that has already fallen into ruin. Rather than focusing on saving kingdoms or gathering allies, the story begins after the apocalypse has already claimed its victims. Players assume the role of a disgraced knight brought back to life by a mysterious force and tasked with uncovering the secrets behind the destruction of the realm. This premise immediately sets a melancholic tone that remains consistent throughout the entire adventure.\n\nThe game's greatest strength lies in its atmosphere. Every environment, from abandoned settlements to gloomy wilderness areas, conveys the feeling of traversing a land that has long since surrendered to despair. Scrolls of the Lord embraces a minimalist approach to storytelling, allowing its setting to communicate much of its lore. There are no grand cinematic sequences or excessive dialogue dumps; instead, the game encourages players to absorb the world through exploration. Fans of classic PC fantasy games will likely appreciate this design philosophy, as it recalls an era when mystery and imagination played a significant role in the player's experience.\n\nCombat combines melee encounters with magical abilities, offering a modest level of flexibility. Swordplay relies on timed strikes and defensive maneuvers, while spells provide an alternative for those who prefer keeping enemies at a distance. The mechanics are simple and occasionally rough around the edges, but they remain functional throughout the campaign. Battles tend to emphasize positioning and caution rather than speed, creating a slower and more deliberate pace. Although veterans of modern action RPGs may find the combat somewhat dated, there is an undeniable charm in its commitment to a more traditional style of gameplay.\n\nProgression is straightforward, keeping the focus on the journey itself rather than on extensive character customization or intricate skill systems. The game's linear structure works in its favor, ensuring that players remain engaged with the unfolding mystery instead of becoming distracted by unnecessary side activities. Exploration still plays an important role, however, as hidden details and environmental cues enrich the narrative and provide context for the world's tragic past. The balance between combat, exploration, and storytelling helps maintain a consistent rhythm across the experience.\n\nThat said, Scrolls of the Lord is unmistakably an independent production, and its limitations are visible. Character models, animations, and enemy variety do not always meet the standards expected from larger releases. Technical shortcomings occasionally interrupt immersion, and some aspects of the presentation feel outdated. Nevertheless, these issues are mitigated by the sincerity of the project. It is evident that Arbatskii Anatolii aimed to create a personal homage to classic fantasy adventures rather than chase mainstream trends, and that passion is present in nearly every aspect of the game.\n\nIn the end, Scrolls of the Lord succeeds as a niche experience tailored to players who value atmosphere and old-school sensibilities over polish and spectacle. It may not appeal to those seeking expansive role-playing systems or cutting-edge visuals, but it offers a memorable journey through a haunting world filled with mystery and decay. For enthusiasts of obscure indie fantasy titles, it stands as an admirable effort that demonstrates how compelling a game can be when it has a clear vision and remains faithful to it.\n\nRating: 6/10",
